{"kind":"assert","text":"H5 closure ruling: approved. The root's named-definition walk keeps the sequenced child as closure bytes under the authenticated installed root, after structural validation and a digest check. It stops there. Pin, birth and turn checks stay closed until H4, which owns sequenced genesis (ruling d18e6933). Root delivery then succeeds and records the child's bytes. Two conditions:\n1. Before H4, an attempt to create the child stops with a typed refusal. It never answers retry, because nothing changes for that definition until a new head lands, so a retry would loop. Say in the H5 Status that H4 turns this refusal into the genesis path.\n2. Mark the retained child in the walk as closure only, so no path other than the closure walk accepts sequenced definitions. derivable keeps refusing them for every other caller. Add a control: the same child sent alone outside a root's closure is still refused.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}